        {
            "id": "kca://review:goodreads/amzn1.gr.review:goodreads.v1.2E0pSS7MyztIPHdoIbH4Nw",
            "reviewer": {
                "id": 120860,
                "name": "Lassarina Aoibhell",
                "url": "/user/show/120860-lassarina-aoibhell",
                "avatar": "",
                "isAuthor": false,
                "reviewCount": 487,
                "followerCount": 5
            },
            "rating": 1,
            "text": "I am DNFing this book on page 84 and we're going to go on a wild ride about why.<br /><br />NB: If you are a scholar of Arthuriana, history, religion, or....well, almost anything, you might want to put down your drink. I'm not responsible for the state of your monitor. <br /><br />We begin with the cover copy: <i>Daughter of a mighty pagan queen, Guinevere has become a threat to her people and a prize to the dreaded sorcerer Merlin. Sent into hiding, she grows up under the protection of a shapeshifting man-wolf and an ornery Druid. But even on the remote coast of Scotland, where dragons feed and watch over her, she is not safe. Merlin knows the young beauty's destiny--and he will stop at nothing to prevent what has been foretold...</i><br /><br />I also need to strongly highlight one of the blurbs. Like, really strongly. \"In addition to being exceptionally well written, [<i>The Dragon Queen</i>] has enormous integrity: All of the stories--of Arthur's parentage, of Merlin, of the characters' daily struggles--are well grounded in the history, politics, and religions of the time.....This is a trilogy to watch.\" --San Jose Mercury News<br /><br />I want you to look at that quote carefully, and then come on a ride with me.<br /><br />I will acknowledge up front my own reading comprehension fail, because I had forgotten that the shapeshifter <i>was</i> actually in the blurb and was yelling about \"what the fuck.\". I'm still saying \"what the fuck,\" just about plenty of other things.<br /><br />Let's start with: It takes until page 48 for Guinevere to show up in any real sense. 500 page book; the main character doesn't actually appear until 10% in. Don't like that. Instead we are following around Dugald, the druid, and Maeniel, the shapeshifter. He's allegedly from Gaul. We begin at Tintagel in the year 470 CE, where Merlin and some of his cronies murder Vortigen. Some kind of magical snake is involved. (Aside: absolutely no problem with Merlin as the villain, particularly in anything allegedly centering the women of this tale.) Maeniel the shifter teleports??? out of the carnage, finds himself a she-wolf who has recently given birth, and shacks up with her. They encounter Dugald and the toddler Guinevere, who is currently disguised as a boy. For some reason, although everyone complains about disliking the Romans, we re-enact the founding of Rome, with Maeniel's mate suckling little Guinevere. Why does the she-wolf agree to this? Who can say. Dugald, meanwhile, talks about the influence of St. Patrick. Yes, that one. \"We don't call ourselves [druids] any longer. Not since Patrick came to Ireland bearing Christ's message.\"<br /><br />....ten pages ago we called Merlin a chief druid. Also. It's the year 470. We don't have exact dates of the saint's life, but we know it was <i>roughly</i> in that century. More importantly, it was the <i>Romans</i> who banned druidic practices, fucking 400 years earlier (Augustus had no chill.) So there is no way that Patrick's actions in Ireland are affecting the order in England. Remember that blurb I highlighted? HMMMM. ALSO WHY ARE YOU, A GUY IN CORNWALL, TALKING ABOUT PATRICK. why. what is even your deal.<br /><br />Also also, in this variation of the story (?), apparently, Dugald had a philosophical disagreement with Merlin and \"lost,\" so he was expected by the rules of his order to commit suicide because he lost.<br /><br />Me: I do not remember reading this in <i>any</i> of the books I consumed during my obsessive period about Celtic histories and cultures and archaeology. (Wiki and checking with knowledgeable friends suggests I am not alone in not remembering this.)<br /><br />If nothing else, that seems like a giant fucking waste of a 20 year apprenticeship, man.<br /><br />(At this point I also stopped to verify my memory that Dugald and Maeniel do not appear in other Arthurian sources. I seem to be correct. And like, yeah, we add characters to stories all the time, I'm just. why are you making me deal with men in a book about Guinevere!)<br /><br />There's also a point where young Guinevere, Dugald, and Maeniel are involved in helping to fight off a ship of slavers, which conveniently results in there being a woman to join the household and teach Guinevere How To Be A Girl. I should specify that the captain of said slaver's ship was apparently named Cymry. Why is he literally named \"Welsh\"? fuck if I know.<br /><br />There's also a particular kind of ambient misogyny that I find tiresome, and like, I know we're in the fifth century and the Romans were assholes, and also I am aware that Celtic cultures were not paragons of equality and virtue, but the available evidence both of Romans being bitchy about how ass-backward Celtic societies were (omg they let their women do things!!!!) and the remnants of brehon law allocating specific property to women after divorce and categorizing a type of marriage as \"she married down and gets to keep all her stuff\" <a href=\"https://celt.ucc.ie//marriage_ei.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\">[wildly paraphrased]</a> I'm just like<br /><br />what. what are you doing, ma'am<br /><br />and once again I'm going to refer back to that blurb. About being well grounded in the history, politics, and religions of the time.<br /><br />I do not recommend actually reading this unless you want to play a drinking game, in which case, may I suggest that the drink you use should perhaps be water?",
