            },
            "rating": 2,
            "text": "I enjoyed Guinevere’s character early on in the book. It was interesting watching her being raised alongside her foster brother, Black Leg, by a wolf shifter (Maniel) and a she-wolf. I do appreciate her relationship with the She Wolf, who Guinevere calls Mother. The thing that really kept me going through this book was Guinevere’s interactions with the dragons of the land and her connection to the land. I was also intrigued by some of the same conversations about religion in this book since Guinevere is raised in nature and has regular interactions with a Druid and some interactions with Christiany.<br />All this being said I struggled with the writing. There are huge chunks of it that are confusing since it’s not always clear who is being talked about when he, she or they are mentioned considering there might be multiple people mentioned previously in the scene. Transitions between moments are also rough. I was questioning when things changed and what was being talked about. The long descriptions of things really didn’t help either and me zoning out as I read. <br />It was also a bit rough to read this because of how things were worded/certain sciences being really uncomfortable for seemingly no reason out of nowhere. The dynamics between Igraine and Merlin’s relationship being one them. Scenes with them were sexual with zero warning and it wasn’t just limited to them. It happened a lot with various characters. Things were also made pretty uncomfortable with minors even if it was a scene that wasn’t intended to uncomfortably sexual, it was made that way by directly saying things like they “were far too young for even a dream of passion.” With how confusing the writing was I couldn’t even tell if there was a reason for why these sort of scenes were written in detail outside of making the audience feel uncomfortable and actively dislike the characters who are at fault for these behaviors.",
            "createdAt": "Sun May 24 2026",
            "updatedAt": "Sun May 24 2026",
